# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_dma_domain_create(vx_handle resource, uint32_t source,
                               vx_handle *out);
int64_t vx_dma_domain_op(vx_handle domain, uint32_t op);
vx_status vx_dma_map(vx_handle domain, vx_handle vmo, uint64_t offset,
                     uint64_t size, uint32_t options, uint64_t *addresses,
                     vx_handle *mapping);
vx_status vx_dma_unmap(vx_handle mapping);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A DmaDomain is what one PCI function may reach by DMA (see the kernel's design, 01 §7.1).
devmgr makes it and keeps it, and gives the function's driver a duplicate that can map, wait for faults and look, but not revoke; so a driver that dies, or lies, cannot leave its device writing into memory that has gone to someone else.

Where an IOMMU covers the function, the domain translates: on x86_64 the kernel drives VT-d, on aarch64 SMMUv3, and in both the IOMMU denies every device all memory until its domain is made.
A mapping then gets device addresses of the domain's own, contiguous however its pages lie, between 4 GiB and 512 GiB.
On x86_64 the firmware's reserved regions for the device are mapped at their own addresses, below that; on aarch64 the GIC ITS's doorbell is, so the device's MSIs arrive.
A function no IOMMU covers gets a pass-through domain, whose device addresses are physical addresses: safe only with devices QEMU emulates.

`vx_dma_domain_create` makes a domain for the PCI function whose requester ID (`bus << 8 | device << 3 | function`) is <source>, with a Resource handle that has MANAGE.
Its handle has MAP, MANAGE, WAIT, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and INSPECT.
A translated function has one domain at a time.
It makes the call `dma_domain_create` with options 0, the only kind so far.

`vx_dma_map` maps the bytes [<offset>, <offset> + <size>) of <vmo> for the device of <domain>, which needs MAP, and writes the device address of each page into `addresses[size / 4096]` and a handle to a DmaMapping for the range into <mapping>.
<offset> and <size> are multiples of 4096; <size> is at most 2 MiB.
<options> says what the device may do, and is checked against the VMO handle's rights: `VX_DMA_READ`, the device reads the memory, needs READ; `VX_DMA_WRITE`, it writes it, needs WRITE; either or both, but not neither.
The VMO must be anonymous memory: not device memory, and not a pager's, whose pages come and go.
The mapping holds the VMO's pages for the device, whatever becomes of the VMO's handles; its own handle has only INSPECT, so it stays with the task that made it.
Each range is a mapping of its own, and a range may be mapped more than once.

`vx_dma_unmap` says the device is done with <mapping>, which needs no right: in a translated domain the range leaves the device's tables, and the IOMMU's caches are invalidated, before its pages are let go.
The wrapper then closes the mapping's handle, whatever the call returned.
A mapping whose handle goes without an unmap (its driver died) is different.
In a translated domain it is unmapped as it goes.
In a pass-through domain nothing can stop the device, so its pages are kept for the device until the domain's owner says the device has been stopped (`VX_DMA_QUIESCED`).

`vx_dma_domain_op` acts on the whole domain:

: `VX_DMA_REVOKE`
  Needs MANAGE.
  Every live mapping becomes the owner's: its handle can no longer unmap it.
  In a translated domain every mapping leaves the device's tables, and its pages are let go, at once; in a pass-through domain they are kept until `VX_DMA_QUIESCED`.
  devmgr revokes a dead driver's domain before it resets the device.
: `VX_DMA_QUIESCED`
  Needs MANAGE.
  The device has been stopped (bus mastering off, the function reset), so every page kept for it is let go.
: `VX_DMA_FAULTS`
  Needs INSPECT.
  Returns how many faults the IOMMU has reported for the device.

A fault also fires a binding on the domain: `port_bind` with `VX_TRIGGER_DMA_FAULT` and WAIT on the domain fires when the count passes the binding's threshold, at once if it already has, with the count as the packet's value.
The domain goes when its last handle and its last mapping have gone.

# EXAMPLES

From `lib/vx-driver/virtio.c`, a virtqueue's three pages given to the device:

```c
vx_status st = vx_vmo_create(QUEUE_BYTES, 0, &vmo);
if (st == VX_OK)
  st = vx_as_map(vx_self, vmo, 0, QUEUE_BYTES, VX_MAP_WRITE, &at);
// kept as long as the driver lives: the device reads the queue and
// writes its used ring
vx_handle mapping;
if (st == VX_OK)
  st = vx_dma_map(v->dma, vmo, 0, QUEUE_BYTES, VX_DMA_READ | VX_DMA_WRITE,
                  pa, &mapping);
vx_handle_close(vmo); // the mappings keep it
```

From the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`), a driver's duplicate of a domain:

```c
CHECK(vx_handle_dup(dom, VX_RIGHT_MAP | VX_RIGHT_WAIT | VX_RIGHT_INSPECT,
                    &user) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_dma_map(user, mem, 0, 8192, VX_DMA_READ | VX_DMA_WRITE, addrs,
                 &map1) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_dma_map(user, mem, 8192, 4096, VX_DMA_WRITE, addrs, &map2) ==
      VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_dma_domain_op(user, VX_DMA_REVOKE) == VX_ERR_ACCESS);
CHECK(vx_dma_domain_op(user, VX_DMA_FAULTS) == 0);
```

